Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
valve surprised the gaming community  this week with the announcement of the  steam deck  a new foray into dedicated pc gaming  hardware  the portable device bears more than a  passing resemblance to nintendo's  popular handheld console hybrid the  nintendo switch  given its general size and form factor  but unlike the switch  the steam deck is built to play your  steam library on the go  and the impressive looking piece of tech  is launching this december  the steam deck comes in three different  versions but each of these offers the  same level of performance  you won't need a more expensive version  in order to play games with more  demanding system requirements  where they differ is in their storage  options there's a 64 gigabyte model  priced at 399 dollars usd  one with 256 gigabytes of storage at 529  and a 512 gigabyte model for 6.49  the pricier models also get some  additional perks the top two models get  a steam community bundle  and the most expensive version comes  with an anti-glare etched screen  but the amount of internal storage is  just part of the story  only the 256 gigabyte and 512 gigabyte  models are equipped with  nvme solid-state drives with the larger  of the two also featuring a faster drive  the 64 gigabyte model by contrast is  equipped with  emmc storage which means the cheapest  steam deck  also has storage that'll be much slower  than you expect  while all three models can have their  storage expanded with a microsd  card buying the cheapest model locks you  into this slower storage solution  so it's worth considering one of the  larger models if you're concerned with  load times  and general usability the steam deck  comes pre-loaded with steamos  a modified version of linux that gives  you a straightforward user interface to  browse  and select games from your steam library  if you're a steam customer  you may already have a wide variety of  games that will be available to play on  steam deck  from the very start but valve has also  emphasized that steam deck is  at its core a pc that means that you can  customize your experience  just like you would on any other pc  including by installing your own  operating system and going outside of  steam for games  hypothetically you could for example  install windows and use that interface  to load up the steam client for your  steam games  and other game clients for other pc  games this may be less elegant than the  built-in steam os  but there's no reason that we know of  why it wouldn't work  the subject of what games you can play  on the steam deck is a situation  that is likely to change between now and  launch and  even after launch as it stands there are  a number of big name  games that aren't supported on linux and  that proton  which valve is using to get certain  games running on linux  doesn't offer a solution for these  include apex legends  destiny 2 and rainbow six siege however  it's entirely possible that support for  these games will come eventually  they may even be supported by the time  steam deck is launched  beyond that there's the issue of storage  space some games are  simply too big to fit on the steam deck  at least in its 64 gigabyte incarnation  you can use a micro sd card to expand  the space and store larger games  but if we're talking about what the  machine can literally play out of the  box  games with larger storage space  requirements won't be compatible  depending on your choice of model as  it's obvious from a single glance  the steam deck strongly resembles the  nintendo switch  with a 720p touchscreen in the middle  and what are essentially halves of a  standard gaming controller  flanking it on either side unlike the  switch's joy-cons  these don't detach from the system  itself so if you want to dock the system  and play on an external display  you'll need a compatible controller the  steam deck can connect to  any standard bluetooth controller though  so you should be able to use any regular  xbox  playstation or third party controller on  the back of the steam deck are four  programmable  grip buttons not unlike what you can  find on the xbox  elite controller on the front the full  size analog sticks are equipped  with capacitive touch sensors that let  the machine know when your fingers are  on them  there are also track pads on both sides  of the screen which  in theory should serve as substitutes  for mouse  input and the steam deck itself has a  gyro sensor built in  for motion controls and a built-in  microphone for voice chat  when using the device you can press the  power button to suspend gameplay  and put the system into sleep mode which  you can then quickly resume from  just like the nintendo switch however  that shouldn't be confused with quick  resume  on the xbox series x and s consoles you  are not able to suspend  multiple games at a time and jump  between them steam deck is equipped with  a 40 watt  hour battery which should top out at 7  to 8 hours of use  by valve's estimate however you can  expect that to decrease  significantly depending on what you're  playing and your chosen graphic settings  this may go as low as 2 hours for the  most intense games  according to what valve told ign in  terms of hardware  the steam deck is impressive in a number  of ways particularly  for the price it remains to be seen how  it will handle a variety of games  particularly if you seek to use features  like ray tracing  but it's worth noting that this has been  designed to facilitate  720p gameplay so the steam deck will  likely not provide  the most high fidelity experience  particularly if you're connecting to a  large external display  the steam deck will include an amd apu  zen 2 cpu  and rdna 2 gpu and it will pack  16 gigabytes of lpddr5 ram  it will also be bigger than the nintendo  switch measuring  298 millimeters wide 117 millimeters  tall  and 49 millimeters thick compare that to  the switch  which is 117 millimeters wide without  attached joy-cons  102 millimeters tall and 13.9  millimeters thick  what's more the steam deck is pretty  heavy valve says it'll weigh about 669  grams  which is about a pound and a half that's  around twice  as heavy as the switch it might be time  to start doing some wrist workouts so  you don't get carpal tunnel  the steam deck supports micro sd cards  for memory expansion  which may be especially necessary if you  purchase the base model that comes with  64 gigabytes of internal storage  valve has also promised that a dock is  coming with price to be announced  which will allow you to plug the device  into your tv or monitor  and play on a bigger screen a lot like  the nintendo switch can  each model also comes with its own  carrying case  although only the most expensive model  includes what valve is calling  an exclusive carrying case other pc  accessories like your mouse  keyboard and what have you will also be  compatible with the steam deck  as will bluetooth controllers and  headphones valve has also confirmed that  you'll be able to use a usbc hub  to hook up more accessories to the steam  deck pre-orders for the steam deck are  now available through steam  and through valve's official website for  the device  for the time being pre-orders are simply  reservations  requiring a five dollar deposit that  will be put toward the final purchase  price at a later date  we're not sure how long valve will keep  pre-orders open so if you want a steam  deck  you should try to lock it in soon to  hear our thoughts on the steam deck  with some analysis about who valve's  device is for and whether it'll compete  with the nintendo switch  watch our breakout from this week's  after dark podcast  you can read even more about the steam  deck on gamespot.com right now and stay  tuned for even more coverage in the  future  and as always if you want to see more be  sure to drop a like  and hit that subscribe button  [Music]  you

